'''''Els Camps, Estret''''' is a city in the [[Cumará (comarca)|Cumará comarca]], [[Estret Province]], [[Balboa]]. With a population of 52,451 in 2026 and an area of 165.8 km², it is the largest municipality in Cumará by area and the second-least densely populated. Els Camps is a rural and exurban agricultural municipality occupying the eastern portion of the comarca, characterised by open farmland, scattered settlements, and the upland fringe around [[Mont Francesc Peak]], the highest point in the comarca. The municipality is served by [[CristoCarril]] [[Line D (CristoCarril)|Line D]] on the Via Sud Subdivision and by [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 9]], which cross the municipality from north to south and west to east respectively.

The city's name means "the fields" in Volisanian, reflecting the agricultural character that has defined the area since before the [[Zong Balboa]] period. The name is shared with several other settlements across Balboa, and the municipality is disambiguated by its province in formal contexts.

== Geography ==

Els Camps occupies the eastern portion of the Cumará comarca, covering 165.8 km² of flat to gently rolling lowland terrain that rises toward the upland fringe in the north. The municipality borders [[Brises]] to the north, [[Varakí]] to the west, and [[La Cruïlla]] to the northwest. To the east, the municipality borders a neighbouring comarca. The terrain is generally flat in the central and southern portions, with a gradual rise toward the northern uplands where [[Mont Francesc Peak]] — the highest point in the Cumará comarca — is located.

The municipality is the largest in the Cumará comarca by area, and its low population density of 316 per km² reflects the predominance of agricultural land use. The built-up area is concentrated in the western town core, near the metro stations and the cathedral, with smaller hamlets scattered across the farmland. The open landscape and rural character distinguish Els Camps sharply from the denser suburban municipalities of the western Cumará corridor.

No expressways serve Els Camps directly. The principal road connections are rural highways linking the town core to [[Varakí]] in the west and [[Brises]] in the north, with secondary roads connecting the scattered hamlets to the central core. The [[CristoCarril]] Via Sud Subdivision rail line passes through the northern portion of the municipality, with a station at Els Camps near the upland fringe. [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 9]] crosses the western portion of the municipality, serving two stations that connect the town core to the regional transit network.

=== Mont Francesc Provincial Park ===

{{main|Mont Francesc Provincial Park}}

[[Mont Francesc Provincial Park]] is a protected natural area centred on [[Mont Francesc Peak]], the highest point in the Cumará comarca, located in the northern portion of the municipality near the border with [[Brises]]. The park covers the peak and its surrounding upland slopes, which are covered with secondary woodland and scrub, contrasting with the open farmland of the lowlands. The peak rises above the surrounding farmland and forms a visible landmark from across the comarca. The park is accessible by public transit via the Els Camps commuter rail station, located a short distance from the park's southern boundary, and is a popular destination for day trips from Sant Cristòfor, particularly during weekends and holidays when the Sant Cristòfor–Haicang line operates limited-stop services to Els Camps.

=== Climate ===

Els Camps has a {{w|tropical monsoon climate}} (Köppen ''Am''), consistent with the southern Estret lowlands. Temperatures are warm year-round, with average daily highs of 30–32 °C and lows of 22–24 °C. The wet season runs from May to November, with the heaviest rainfall from June to August. The open, flat terrain and absence of significant urban heat island effects result in slightly cooler nighttime temperatures than the built-up suburbs of the western corridor. The upland area around Mont Francesc Peak experiences marginally cooler temperatures and higher rainfall than the lowlands.

== History ==

=== Zong Balboa period (1899–1950) ===

{{main|Zong Balboa}}

The land that became Els Camps was agricultural territory throughout the [[Zong Balboa]] period, producing food crops for the Sant Cristòfor market. The area was sparsely populated, with scattered farmsteads and a small village core serving as the local market centre. The Via Sud Subdivision rail line, built during the Zong period to connect Sant Cristòfor to the southern canal zone, passed through the northern portion of the farmland near the upland fringe. A station was constructed at Els Camps to serve the agricultural community, providing a freight connection for crop shipments and a limited passenger service for local residents.

The [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1947) did not directly affect the Els Camps farmland, which continued to produce food for the regional market throughout the war years. The rural population remained stable, as the agricultural economy was less disrupted by the fighting than the urban centres.

=== AC mandate and post-reunification (1950–present) ===

{{main|International City of Areza}}

The [[Adonian Community]] mandate period and the subsequent post-reunification era brought gradual change to Els Camps. The construction of the [[International City of Areza]] and the growth of the southern Estret corridor increased demand for agricultural land, as developers looked toward the open fields of the eastern comarca for future residential expansion. The municipal government, established in 1961, adopted planning policies that preserved the agricultural character of the municipality while permitting limited residential development around the existing town core.

The extension of [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 9]] through the western portion of the municipality in the post-reunification period brought direct rapid transit service to Els Camps for the first time. Two stations were constructed — El Camps, serving the town core, and Carretera Maria, serving the southern approach to the town — connecting Els Camps residents directly to the Sant Cristòfor metro network and, via interchange, to the broader regional rail system. The metro connection moderated the pressure for suburban development, as residents could commute to Sant Cristòfor without relying on road connections, but the municipal government maintained its restrictive planning policies and the agricultural character of the municipality was largely preserved.

In the 2000s and 2010s, the municipality experienced modest population growth as some residents sought a rural lifestyle within commuting distance of Sant Cristòfor and Areza. This exurban growth was concentrated in the western town core, near the metro stations, and along the road connections to Varakí and La Cruïlla. The agricultural land use remained dominant, and the municipal government continued to restrict large-scale residential development.

== Government ==

Els Camps is governed by an elected {{w|mayor}} and a municipal council. The mayor serves four-year terms and has executive authority over municipal administration, planning, and local services. The current mayor is [[Joana Riera i Puig]] of the [[Front Socialista de Balboa]] (FSB), elected in 2024. Riera i Puig, a former agricultural cooperative director, campaigned on protecting farmland from development pressure, improving rural road maintenance, and enhancing municipal bus connections to the metro and commuter rail stations. The FSB's strength in Els Camps reflects the rural, working-class character of the municipality and the agricultural community's preference for collective services and land-use protection.

== Demographics ==

Els Camps's population of 52,451 (2026) makes it the fifth-largest municipality in the Cumará comarca by population, despite being the largest by area. The population density of 316 per km² is the second-lowest in the comarca, reflecting the predominance of agricultural land use. The population is concentrated in the western town core, near the metro stations and the cathedral, with smaller settlements scattered across the farmland.

The demographic profile of Els Camps differs from the suburban municipalities of the western corridor. The population is younger than the comarca average, with a larger proportion of families employed in agriculture and related industries. [[Mixed Balboan people|Mixed Balboans]] constitute the majority of the population, reflecting the rural agricultural demographic. [[Black Balboan people|Black Balboans]] are present in smaller numbers than in the suburban municipalities, concentrated in the town core. The [[Fosian Balboan people|Fosian]] community is small, centred around the Buda d'Hiram temple in the eastern portion of the municipality. Household incomes are below the comarca average, consistent with the rural agricultural economy.

== Economy ==

Els Camps's economy is primarily agricultural. The municipality's farmland produces food crops for the Sant Cristòfor and Areza markets, including rice, maize, root vegetables, and tropical fruits. Smaller-scale livestock farming and dairy production supplement the crop economy. The agricultural sector is organised through cooperatives, several of which date from the post-reunification period when collective farming structures were established.

The town core contains the principal commercial and service businesses, including agricultural supply stores, food processing facilities, and retail shops serving the local population. The municipality has minimal industrial activity, limited to small-scale food processing and agricultural equipment repair. The exurban population that has settled in the municipality in recent decades commutes to Sant Cristòfor, Areza, or the suburban corridor via the metro, but this group remains a minority of the total population.

== Transport ==

Els Camps's transport network combines limited rail and metro connections with rural road infrastructure.

=== Commuter rail ===

The [[CristoCarril]] [[Line D (CristoCarril)|Line D]] operates on the Via Sud Subdivision, which passes through the northern portion of Els Camps. The line has one station within the municipality — Els Camps station, located near the upland fringe in the northern part of the municipality — providing a commuter rail connection to Sant Cristòfor to the north and to the southern Estret corridor. The Sant Cristòfor–[[Haicang]] line also uses the Via Sud Subdivision, offering a cross-isthmus service between the Shendan and Aethiopian coasts. On weekends and holidays, the Sant Cristòfor–Haicang line operates limited-stop services that call at Els Camps, providing a direct connection for visitors to [[Mont Francesc Provincial Park]] and reducing travel time to Sant Cristòfor and Haicang. Service on the Via Sud Subdivision is less frequent than on the busier Portnou and Coastal corridors, reflecting the lower population density of the eastern comarca.

=== Metro ===

[[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro Line 9]] crosses the western portion of the municipality, serving two stations within Els Camps. El Camps station, located in the town core, is the primary metro access point for the municipality. Carretera Maria station, located south of the town core, serves the southern approach and the agricultural areas between Els Camps and [[Varakí]]. The metro connection provides direct access to the Sant Cristòfor metro network, with interchange to CristoCarril services at stations in neighbouring municipalities. Line 9 also connects Els Camps to [[Varakí]] and the HSR station there, providing an alternative to road travel for commuters to Areza.

=== Bus ===

The ''Autobus d'Els Camps'' (AdEC) is the municipal bus network operated by the city government. The network consists of three lines serving the town core and the principal hamlets, with connections to the metro and commuter rail stations. The primary route connects the Els Camps train station in the north to the El Camps metro station in the town core, providing a link between the two rail services. The secondary route connects the town core to the scattered hamlets in the eastern and southern portions of the municipality. The tertiary route connects the town core to [[Brises]] in the north, with onward connections to the CristoCarril network. Service frequency is limited, with several departures per day on each route, reflecting the low demand and rural character of the municipality.

=== Roads ===

Els Camps is served by rural highways rather than expressways. The principal road is the highway linking the town core to [[Varakí]] in the west, which connects to the expressway network at the Varakí interchange. A secondary road links the town core to [[Brises]] in the north. The road network within the municipality consists of rural roads connecting the scattered hamlets to the town core, with limited paving and maintenance compared to the suburban municipalities.

== Neighbourhoods ==

Els Camps is divided into three municipal zones, reflecting the dispersed settlement pattern and the influence of the rail and metro corridors:

=== Zona 1 — Central ===

'''Zona 1''' is the town core and principal settlement of the municipality, located in the western portion of Els Camps near the Metro Line 9 stations. The town core contains the municipal government offices, the [[Catedral del Sant Rosari|Catedral del Sant Rosari d'Els Camps]], the central market, the principal commercial businesses, and the El Camps metro station. The housing stock consists of low-rise buildings, with a mix of traditional farmhouses, modest detached houses, and small apartment buildings. The town core is the most densely settled part of the municipality, but even here the density is low compared to the suburban municipalities of the western corridor. The exurban growth of the 2000s and 2010s has been concentrated in Zona 1, with new housing developments on the edge of the town core near the metro stations.

=== Zona 2 — Nord ===

'''Zona 2''' is the northern portion of the municipality, centred on the Els Camps commuter rail station and the upland area around [[Mont Francesc Provincial Park]]. The zone is the least densely settled part of the municipality, with scattered farmsteads and woodland covering the rising terrain toward the comarca's highest point. The Els Camps train station, located near the upland fringe, serves the surrounding agricultural community and provides a rail connection to Sant Cristòfor and the southern corridor. The station also provides transit access to the provincial park, particularly during weekends and holidays when the Sant Cristòfor–Haicang line operates limited-stop services calling at Els Camps.

=== Zona 3 — Rural ===

'''Zona 3''' encompasses the eastern and southern portions of the municipality, including the scattered hamlets, farmsteads, and agricultural land that make up the bulk of Els Camps. The hamlets are small clusters of houses centred on crossroads or former estate centres, with populations ranging from a few dozen to a few hundred. The [[Buda d'Hiram]] [[Baiduism|Baiduist]] temple, located in the eastern portion of the zone, serves the small Fosian community and is a notable local landmark. The housing stock is predominantly traditional, with farmhouses and agricultural workers' cottages scattered across the farmland. The rural zone has seen limited modern development, as the municipal government has restricted residential construction to preserve agricultural land use.

== Notable places ==

* '''[[Catedral del Sant Rosari]]''' — the Catholic cathedral in the town core, serving as the principal place of worship for the Campenc community. The cathedral is the seat of the parish of Els Camps and a focal point of the town core.
* '''[[Buda d'Hiram]]''' — a [[Baiduism|Baiduist]] temple in the eastern portion of the municipality, serving the small Fosian community of Els Camps and the surrounding rural area. The temple is a notable local landmark, reflecting the religious diversity of the eastern Cumará farmland.
* '''[[Mont Francesc Provincial Park]]''' — a protected natural area centred on Mont Francesc Peak, the highest point in the Cumará comarca. The park is accessible via the Els Camps commuter rail station and is a popular day-trip destination from Sant Cristòfor.
* '''Els Camps station''' — the CristoCarril Line D commuter rail station on the Via Sud Subdivision, located in the northern portion of the municipality near the upland fringe.
* '''El Camps metro station''' — the Metro Line 9 station in the town core, providing the primary rapid transit connection to Sant Cristòfor.
* '''Carretera Maria metro station''' — the Metro Line 9 station south of the town core, serving the southern approach to Els Camps.

